{
 "what": "An outside check of OpenAI’s conditional Lean proof that prime gaps are at most 186, re-derived from the facts in corpus/prime-gaps-186 by tools/record-prime-gaps-186.js.",
 "undecided": "The bound liminf (p_{n+1} − p_n) ≤ 186, its proof, the Lean development and the numerical certificate are OpenAI’s (GPT-6 Astra; openai/PrimeGaps186, 30 August 2026). The Lean proof is conditional on three axioms it declares. This record does not verify the bound 186 and proves none of the three axioms.",
 "gaps": [
  {
   "id": "conditional",
   "b": "This does not verify the bound 186.",
   "text": "The Lean proof shows: if three assumptions hold, then 186. Nothing here proves the assumptions."
  },
  {
   "id": "finite",
   "b": "Two assumptions are theorems quoted from the literature.",
   "text": "A bound on a rank-three Kloosterman sum, after Deligne and Katz, and a correlation bound of Fouvry, Kowalski and Michel. In Lean they are assumed for every prime. Every instance was decided for the primes below 2,000, and nothing above."
  },
  {
   "id": "majorant",
   "b": "The third assumption is about integrals; every program computes estimates.",
   "text": "It asserts 149 upper bounds for integrals and three more bounds. OpenAI’s certificate, and the recomputation here, evaluate upper estimates of the 149 integrals. That each integral is at most its estimate is argued in OpenAI’s note and re-derived by hand in four AI sessions here; it is not in Lean. Its base is: OpenAI’s Lean proves that the underlying law is a probability measure with the expected density. The chain from each integral to its estimate is what is missing."
  },
  {
   "id": "reading",
   "b": "One reading choice.",
   "text": "For 61 of the 149 rows the note picks an ingredient by a test whose outcome it does not print. The reading was fixed before anything was compared; under the other reading two rows would exceed their printed bound. The values match OpenAI’s own intervals under the reading taken."
  },
  {
   "id": "sessions",
   "b": "The recomputation and its review were done by AI sessions.",
   "text": "Under one person’s direction, with the rules sealed first. No human referee has read the hand derivations."
  },
  {
   "id": "tools",
   "b": "Two defects of our own statement-checking tool surfaced on the way.",
   "text": "An absent checker was counted as a kernel that did not accept, and two generated files did not build under the release’s linter option. Both were fixed in public before the records they touched were derived; neither changed a prediction."
  }
 ],
